</history><permissions><!--&#x000a9; 2025 Wiley Periodicals LLC.--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2024 The Author(s). <italic toggle="yes">Journal of Clinical Ultrasound</italic> published by Wiley Periodicals LLC.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited, the use is non&#x02010;commercial and no modifications or adaptations are made.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:JCU-53-421.pdf"/><abstract><title>ABSTRACT</title><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0001"><title>Introduction</title><p>Right diaphragmatic excursion is a reliable and reproducible technique used in intensive care to assess diaphragmatic function. The aim of this study was to investigate the relationship between the appearance of diaphragmatic motion and the etiological diagnosis of patients admitted to the emergency department with acute respiratory failure (ARF).</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0002"><title>Materials</title><p>A prospective, observational, and multicenter pilot study was conducted. All adult patients admitted in the emergency department with ARF were included. The different visual patterns of diaphragmatic motion were analyzed according to the three main etiologies of ARF encountered in emergency departments.</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0003"><title>Results</title><p>A total of 39 adult patients were included. We observed a different visual pattern in patients with pneumonia. A sum of plateau times of less than 0.2&#x02009;s predicted that the main diagnosis was pneumonia, with sensitivity&#x02009;=&#x02009;89% 95%CI [52%; 100%], specificity&#x02009;=&#x02009;87% 95%CI [69%; 96%].</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0004"><title>Conclusion</title><p>Our study seems to show that the shape of diaphragmatic motion in patients with ARF secondary to pneumonia is different from that in patients with exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or acute heart failure.</p><p>
<bold>Trial Registration:</bold>
<ext-link xlink:href="http://clinicaltrials.gov" ext-link-type="uri">ClinicalTrials.gov</ext-link>: NCT04591509</p></sec></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical"><p>In this prospective bicentric study, we studied the relationship between the appearance of diaphragmatic motion and the etiological diagnosis of patients admitted to the emergency department with ARF. We observed a different visual pattern in patients with pneumonia. This is an interesting result as it allows the diagnosis of pneumonia without the need for any measurements.<boxed-text position="anchor" content-type="graphic" id="jcu23886-blkfxd-0001"><graphic xlink:href="JCU-53-421-g005.jpg" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></boxed-text>
</p></abstract><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="jcu23886-kwd-0001">acute heart failure</kwd><kwd id="jcu23886-kwd-0002">acute respiratory failure</kwd><kwd id="jcu23886-kwd-0003">diaphragmatic motion</kwd><kwd id="jcu23886-kwd-0004">emergency department</kwd><kwd id="jcu23886-kwd-0005">exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease</kwd><kwd id="jcu23886-kwd-0006">pneumonia</kwd></kwd-group><counts><fig-count count="5"/><table-count count="2"/><page-count count="8"/><word-count count="4400"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>March/April 2025</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.5.4 mode:remove_FC converted:14.03.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body id="jcu23886-body-0001"><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0005"><label>1</label><title>Introduction</title><p>Acute respiratory failure (ARF) is frequently encountered in emergency departments (EDs) and is a real challenge for physicians [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>]. Acute decompensated heart failure (ADHF), acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (AECOPD), and pneumonia are the most common diagnoses [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]. The main objective of the emergency physician (EP) is to identify the most appropriate diagnosis and initiate suitable therapy. Anamnesis and clinical examination are fundamental to the management of ARF and can lead to the identification of a specific etiology. However, the atypical clinical presentation, particularly in the elderly, makes diagnosis difficult [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>]. As a result, initial treatment is empirical, leading to prolonged hospital stays and a significant increase in mortality [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>].</p><p>The diagnostic value of point&#x02010;of&#x02010;care ultrasound (POCUS) has been studied for many years [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>]. It is frequently used in intensive care units to measure right diaphragmatic excursion (RDE) during a respiratory cycle. Evaluation of RDE enables respiratory muscle fatigue to be estimated by assessing diaphragmatic function [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>]. This technique appears reliable and reproducible, with high inter&#x02010;rater reliability [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>]. Analysis of RDE in time&#x02013;motion mode (M&#x02010;mode) is useful for predicting prolonged or difficult ventilatory weaning in ventilated patients [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>]. For spontaneously ventilated patients, this technique seems to predict the subsequent need for mechanical ventilation for a specific category of patients [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>]. In a normal state, the diaphragm contributes to three&#x02010;quarters of the inspiratory volume of the vital capacity [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. Also, inspiratory time (IT) and expiratory time (ET) seem to differ according to the etiological diagnosis [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>]. For example, the IT of airway diseases would appear to be different from that of parenchymal lung diseases. IT and ET differences, and the presence or absence of an inspiratory and expiratory plateaus result in differences in the appearance of the diaphragmatic motion analyzed in M&#x02010;mode. The analysis of diaphragmatic motion could be relevant for etiological diagnosis, complementing the assessment of respiratory exhaustion.</p><p>The aim of this study was to investigate the relationship between the appearance of diaphragmatic motion and the etiological diagnosis of patients admitted to the ED with ARF.</p></sec><sec sec-type="materials-and-methods" id="jcu23886-sec-0006"><label>2</label><title>Materials and Methods</title><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0007"><label>2.1</label><title>Study Design</title><p>This was a prospective, multicenter, observational study conducted from December 2019 to June 2020 in the ED of the University Hospital of N&#x000ee;mes and European Hospital of Paris (Georges Pompidou Hospital). This study was carried out in accordance with the amended Declaration of Helsinki. In accordance with French law [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>], the study protocol was approved by the Comit&#x000e9; de protection des personnes (CPP Ouest 1, ref. 2019T3&#x02010;08) and verbal informed consent was obtained from all study subjects. If it was not possible to obtain the patient's consent, then the consent of family members or the trusted support person was sought. The study was pre&#x02010;registered on <ext-link xlink:href="http://clinicaltrials.gov" ext-link-type="uri">ClinicalTrials.gov</ext-link>.</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0008"><label>2.2</label><title>Population</title><p>All adult patients with ARF were eligible for enrollment only when a study EP was available. ARF was defined by the association of a respiratory rate&#x02009;&#x02265;&#x02009;25&#x02009;min<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> and hypoxemia (peripheral oxygen saturation [SpO2]&#x02009;&#x02264;&#x02009;90% or arterial partial pressure of oxygen (PaO2)&#x02009;&#x02264;&#x02009;70&#x02009;mmHg in breathing room air) and/or hypercarbia (arterial partial pressure of CO<sub>2</sub> [PaCO2]&#x02009;&#x02265;&#x02009;45&#x02009;mmHg, with pH &#x02264;&#x02009;7.35). Patients could be enrolled if they were at least 18&#x02009;years of age, were not pregnant, were affiliated or beneficiary of a health&#x02010;insurance plan, had not previously been included in other studies, and were admitted for the primary reason for ARF. Patients were not included if they were admitted with ARF requiring immediate mechanical ventilation or if they were admitted with current respiratory assistance therapy.</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0009"><label>2.3</label><title>Data Collection and Measurements</title><p>Epidemiological data collected included sex, age, medical history of cardiovascular or respiratory problems, vital signs on hospital admission, and arterial blood gas analysis as needed.</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0010"><label>2.4</label><title>Ultrasound (US) Techniques</title><p>US analysis of diaphragmatic motion was performed at the patient's admission by EPs who were informed about the protocol. All EPs were certified with a university diploma in POCUS and had either used this technique in clinical practice or the previous study [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]. US scans were performed with a conventional US device (Vivid S60, GE Healthcare, Medical System, Israel) and a phased&#x02010;array probe (1.3&#x02013;3.3&#x02009;MHz). This study used the same technique as that described and validated in previous studies [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>]. Briefly, the patient is placed in a semi&#x02010;recumbent position and the probe is placed in the sub&#x02010;costal area between the medioclavicular line and the anterior axillary line to obtain the best imaging of the left hemidiaphragmatic dome. The measurement is made during tidal breathing. No breathing maneuver was required from the patient. The liver is used as a window for the right hemidiaphragm. The probe was oriented transversely (the probe marker was oriented laterally), angled into the chest wall and was ventral, so that the US beam reached perpendicularly the posterior third of the right hemidiaphragm. An abdominal preset was used. The operator switched on M&#x02010;mode tracing when an angle of more than 70&#x000b0; was achieved between the uppermost part of the diaphragm and the analysis axis in the most cranial part of diaphragm. Following these procedures increases measurement accuracy and inter&#x02010;observer reproducibility. The picture was frozen at a depth of 15&#x02013;20&#x02009;cm and a scan speed of approximately 15&#x02009;mm/s. Six diaphragmatic motions were recorded in order to calculate the average of the different measurements. RDE is defined by the distance (cm) between the maximal inspiratory and expiratory position of the diaphragm (diaphragm amplitude) (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). Inspiratory plateau time (IPT) is the time between the end of the upward slope and the beginning of the downward slope of the diaphragmatic motion (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). IT is defined as the rise time of the diaphragmatic motion and the IPT (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). Expiratory plateau time (EPT) is the time between the end of the descent and the start of the ascent of the diaphragmatic motion (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). ET is defined by the time of descent of the diaphragmatic motion and the EPT (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). The sum of plateau times (SPT) represents the addition of the IPT and the EPT. Finally, the I/E ratio corresponds to the ratio between IT and ET. Images and measurements were recorded on the US system using the patient's inclusion number.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jcu23886-fig-0001"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>Ultrasound analysis of diaphragmatic motion and various recorded measurements. (A) Ultrasound image of diaphragmatic motion. (B) Schematic illustration of diaphragmatic motion with the various measurements performed. EPT: expiratory plateau time; ET: expiratory time; IPT: inspiratory plateau time; IT: inspiratory time; RDE: right diaphragmatic excursion.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCU-53-421-g004" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3"/></fig></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0011"><label>2.5</label><title>Outcomes</title><p>The final diagnosis was made without knowledge of the POCUS results. Two judging physicians (an EP and a critical care physician) judged the final diagnosis based on a pre&#x02010;established list: ADHF, AECOPD, and pneumonia. Each referee could provide two diagnoses per patient. Each referee was blind to the judgment of the other. They judged the final diagnosis on the whole hospital medical record except the results of the POCUS (clinical examination, imaging, biology, evolution). If the final diagnosis did not agree between the two referees, then an adjudication committee composed of two other judging physicians (an EP and a critical care physician) met to judge the final diagnosis. The adjudication committee also judged the final diagnosis based on the entire hospital medical record without the POCUS data.</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0012"><label>2.6</label><title>Endpoints</title><p>The main objective of this study was to analyze the visual pattern of diaphragmatic motion according to the etiological diagnosis of ARF among the three main diagnoses (ADHF, AECOPD, and pneumonia).</p><p>Secondary objectives focused on the performance of different measures calculated from diaphragmatic motion (IPT, EPT, SPT, I/E ratio). Considering the final diagnosis made by the referees as the standard criterion, the diagnostic performance of each measure was calculated.</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0013"><label>2.7</label><title>Number of Subjects</title><p>No previous study has analyzed the visual pattern of diaphragmatic motion according to the etiology of ARF. Moreover, according to the exploratory nature of the research, none of the criteria analyzed was chosen as the primary endpoint. Therefore, no sample size calculation was possible.</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0014"><label>2.8</label><title>Data Analysis</title><p>The quantitative variables are expressed as medians and 25th and 75th percentiles. Qualitative data are expressed by their number and percentage. The sensitivity (Sn), specificity (Sp), positive predictive value (PPV), negative predictive value (NPV), positive likelihood ratio (PLR), and negative likelihood ratio (NLR) of the various parameters for the diagnosis of ARF etiology were calculated using the exact binomial method and 95% confidence interval (95%CI), and the area under the ROC curve was calculated. All statistical analysis was performed with R 4.0.2 (R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria).</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="jcu23886-sec-0015"><label>3</label><title>Results</title><p>In this study, 67 patients were eligible and 39 (58%) were included (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2</xref>, Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>). The main diagnosis is shown in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>, where 9 (23%) patients had an associated diagnosis. For 7 (18%) patients, the adjudication committee was needed to establish the final diagnosis.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jcu23886-fig-0002"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>Study flow chart. US: ultrasound; DM: diaphragmatic motion.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCU-53-421-g001" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-5"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="jcu23886-tbl-0001" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Baseline characteristics of the population.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Variables</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Values</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age (years)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">77 [65; 89]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Women</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17 (44%)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BMI</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25 [23; 28]</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1">History of</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Heart failure</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 (21%)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">COPD</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17 (44%)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Asthma</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (10%)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lung cancer</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 (8%)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1">Initial vital signs</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Heart rate (bpm)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">88 [78; 93]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SBP (mmHg)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">137 [115; 154]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Respiratory rate (min<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">26 [22; 28]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SpO<sub>2</sub> (%)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">92% [87%; 95%]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Glasgow scale</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15 [15; 15]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Temperature (&#x000b0;C)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">37 [36.4; 38]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ventilation during the first 4&#x02009;h</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (26%)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1">Main diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AECOPD</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">19 (49%)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ADHF</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11 (28%)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pneumonia</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (23%)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1">US results</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">RDE (cm)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.9 [1.1; 2.4]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IPT (s)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.1 [0; 0.2]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EPT (s)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.4 [0.1; 0.6]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SPT (s)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.6 [0.2; 0.7]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">I/E ratio</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.28 [0.24; 0.45]</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="jcu23886-ntgp-0002"><fn id="jcu23886-note-0002"><p>
<italic toggle="yes">Note</italic>: Data are expressed as median [Q1; Q3] or number (%).</p></fn><fn id="jcu23886-note-0003"><p>Abbreviations: ADHF: acute decompensated heart failure; AECOPD: acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; BMI: body mass index; COPD: chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; EPT: expiratory plateau time; I/E ratio: ratio between inspiratory time and expiratory time; IPT: inspiratory plateau time; RDE: right diaphragmatic excursion; SBP: systolic blood pressure; SpO2: oxygen saturation levels measured by pulse oximeter; SPT: sum of plateau times.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3</xref> shows each visual pattern of diaphragmatic motion as a function of final diagnosis. In contrast to ADHF and AECOPD, the visual pattern of diaphragmatic motion in pneumonia has a sinusoidal appearance, with very short plateau times.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jcu23886-fig-0003"><label>FIGURE 3</label><caption><p>Visual pattern of diaphragmatic motion according to final diagnosis. (A) Ultrasound images of diaphragmatic motion. (B) Graphic representations of diaphragmatic motion according to final diagnosis. ADHF: acute decompensated heart failure; AECOPD: acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCU-53-421-g002" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-7"/></fig><p>The values of the IPT, EPT, SPT, and I/E ratio as a function of principal diagnosis are shown in Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4</xref>. Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jcu23886-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref> shows the diagnostic performance of various measures for each main diagnosis. The criterion with the optimum performance for the etiological diagnosis of AECOPD was the I/E ratio. The criterion with the optimum performance for the etiological diagnosis of ADHF was the EPT. The criterion with the optimum performance for the etiological diagnosis of pneumonia was the SPT.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jcu23886-fig-0004"><label>FIGURE 4</label><caption><p>Values for IPT, EPT, SPT, and I/E ratio according to main diagnosis. ADHF: acute decompensated heart failure; AECOPD: acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; EPT: expiratory plateau time; I/E ratio: ratio between inspiratory time and expiratory time; IPT: inspiratory plateau time; SPT: sum of plateau times.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCU-53-421-g003" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-9"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="jcu23886-tbl-0002" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 2</label><caption><p>Diagnostic performance of various measures for each main diagnosis.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AUC</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Optimal threshold</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sn</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sp</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">PPV</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NPV</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">PLR</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NLR</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td colspan="9" align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1">Acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.62 [0.44; 0.79]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">42% [20; 67]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">80% [56; 94]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">67% [39; 85]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">59% [34; 86]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.11 [0.76; 5.86]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.72 [0.47; 1.13]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.57 [0.39; 0.75]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.1</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100% [82; NaN]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20% [6; 44]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54% [22; NaN]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100% [50; 100]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.25 [1; 1.56]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.62 [0.44; 0.80]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.1</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100% [82; NaN]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20% [6; 44]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54% [22; NaN]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100% [50; 100]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.25 [1; 1.56]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">I/E ratio</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.72 [0.55; 0.88]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.27</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">79% [54; 94]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60% [36; 81]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">65% [41; 89]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">75% [49; 89]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.97 [1.1; 3.54]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.35 [0.14; 0.9]</td></tr><tr><td colspan="9" align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1">Acute decompensated heart failure</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.63 [0.44; 0.82]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.1</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">82% [48; 98]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">46% [28; 66]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">38% [21; 85]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">87% [57; 94]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.53 [0.98; 2.38]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.39 [0.11; 1.46]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.76 [0.61; 0.91]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.3</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100% [72; NaN]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54% [34; 73]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">46% [27; NaN]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100% [77; 100]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.15 [1.45; 3.21]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.72 [0.56; 0.88]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.5</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">91% [59; 100]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57% [37; 76]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">46% [27; 97]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">94% [70; 97]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.12 [1.33; 3.38]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.16 [0.02; 1.06]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">I/E ratio</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.61 [0.40; 0.81]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.27</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">64% [31; 89]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61% [41; 79]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">39% [22; 75]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">81% [52; 91]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.62 [0.85; 3.08]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.6 [0.26; 1.38]</td></tr><tr><td colspan="9" align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1">Pneumonia</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.81 [0.69; 0.94]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">78% [40; 97]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">77% [58; 90]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50% [29; 91]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">92% [69; 97]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.33 [1.60; 6.96]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.29 [0.08; 1]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.89 [0.78; 1]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.2</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">89% [52; 100]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">77% [58; 90]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">53% [32; 98]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">96% [76; 98]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.81 [1.91; 7.58]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.15 [0.02; 0.93]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SPT</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.91 [0.81; 1]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.2</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">89% [52; 100]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">87% [69; 96]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">67% [41; 99]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">96% [78; 99]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.67 [2.6; 17.09]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.13 [0.02; 0.82]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">I/E ratio</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.68 [0.50; 0.87]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.29</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">89% [52; 100]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">53% [34; 72]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36% [21; 96]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">94% [68; 97]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.91 [1.22; 2.98]</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.21 [0.03; 1.36]</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="jcu23886-ntgp-0003"><fn id="jcu23886-note-0004"><p>Abbreviations: AUC: area under the curve; EPT: expiratory plateau time; I/E ratio: ratio between inspiratory time and expiratory time; IPT: inspiratory plateau time; NaN: not a number; NLR: negative likelihood ratio; NPV: negative predictive value; PLR: positive likelihood ratio; PPV: positive predictive value; Sn: sensitivity; Sp: specificity; SPT: sum of plateau times.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="jcu23886-sec-0016"><label>4</label><title>Discussion</title><p>In this study, we observed a different visual pattern in patients with pneumonia. In contrast to AECOPD and ADHF, the visual pattern of diaphragmatic motion of pneumonia has a sinusoidal appearance, with very short plateau times. From a practical standpoint, this is an interesting result as it allows the diagnosis of pneumonia to be established without the need for any measurements. Analysis of the SPT confirms this particular visual appearance, with an AUC&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.91. With an optimal cut&#x02010;off&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.2&#x02009;s, the Sn and Sp can be considered acceptable (89% and 87%, respectively). These results are consistent with the specific sinusoidal appearance of diaphragmatic motion and with the pathophysiology of these pathologies [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>]. Moreover, the EPT seems to have the greatest influence on the pneumonia diagnosis. The existence of an EPT&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.1&#x02009;s rules out the diagnoses of AECOPD and ADHF. Only patients with pneumonia can have a null EPT. It is known that in the ventilatory mechanics of patients with ADHF or AECOPD, there is a prolonged ET [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>]. The fact that during this time the diaphragm moves very little (plateau) is consistent. US analysis of diaphragmatic movements seems to be suitable to measure respiratory times accurately. Because diagnostic accuracy in dyspneic patients has an impact on mortality [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>], improving diagnostic relevance with a tool (US) already used in emergency medicine and in dyspneic patients seems interesting. However, the diagnostic performance of lung US for the diagnosis of pneumonia is already good [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>]. If further studies confirm the value of diaphragmatic motion shape analysis in the diagnosis of pneumonia, then its added value over and above that of lung US should also be investigated.</p><p>Many patients in the ED, up to more than 20% especially in elderly patients [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], have mixed causes of dyspnea. In our study, the ARF causes of pneumopathy associated with ADHF or AECOPD appear visually to have an expiratory plateau. Therefore, it seems difficult to make the diagnosis of pneumonia when associated with another diagnosis based on visualization of diaphragmatic motion alone. However, after diuretic therapy for ADHF or aerosol therapy for AECOPD, it may be worthwhile to perform a further visual analysis of diaphragmatic motion to rule out associated pneumonia.</p><p>Analysis of the different diaphragmatic motion times (IPT, EPT, SPT, and I/E ratio) suggests that only EPT and SPT are relevant to the diagnosis of ARF. The diagnostic performance of IPT seems very moderate independent of the etiological diagnosis and the I/E ratio is only of interest for pneumonia. Moreover, the excellent performance of cardiopulmonary US for the diagnosis of ADHF [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>] suggests that this technique may not have any added value outside of mixed causes. Finally, the visual and respiratory time analysis is not efficient for the diagnosis of AECOPD. We would have expected a different expiratory aspect to ADHF, which we do not find in this analysis. This may be secondary to the number of patients included in this study or to the inability of diaphragmatic movements to show the difference in respiratory mechanics between the two etiologies.</p><p>Most studies have focused on the analysis of RDE to assess respiratory muscle fatigue [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>]. In a normal state, the diaphragm contributes to three&#x02010;quarters of the inspiratory volume of the vital capacity [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. It is therefore logical to assume that the inspiratory and ETs of diaphragmatic motion are altered in the presence of pulmonary disease [<xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="jcu23886-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>]. The originality of this study was to analyze the visual pattern of diaphragmatic motion according to the main causes of ARF in the ED. The measurements of different diaphragmatic motion times provide a better understanding of the visual pattern observed.</p><p>Nevertheless, our study has its limits. First, it lacks statistical power, which is reflected in the interpretation of the results by the width of our confidence intervals and which explains the absence of results enabling a diagnosis to be affirmed (VPP, Sp). Second, the extraction and measurement of indices was carried out by a single investigator, which may constitute a bias due to the absence of proofreading. Finally, only three etiologies were studied, which do not reflect the full range of diagnoses that can be encountered in an ED. Consequently, it lacks diversity and generalizability.</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="jcu23886-sec-0017"><label>5</label><title>Conclusion</title><p>Our study seems to show a potential value of diaphragmatic movement analysis in patients admitted to the ED with ARF. Its visual analysis appears to be highly specific for patients with pneumonia and very short EPT and SPT. However, this is a preliminary study and these results need to be confirmed by larger&#x02010;scale studies. This simple, reproducible method deserves to be considered in future studies to improve the diagnostic performance of EPs.</p></sec><sec id="jcu23886-sec-0018"><title>Ethics Statement</title><p>According to French law, the study protocol was approved by the Comit&#x000e9; de protection des personnes (CPP Ouest 1, ref. 2019T3&#x02010;08).</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="jcu23886-sec-0019"><title>Consent</title><p>Patients were informed about the study and verbal consent was obtained.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="jcu23886-sec-0020"><title>Conflicts of Interest</title><p>T.M. and X.B. declare a competing interest as US teachers for GE (GE MEDICAL SYSTEMS ULTRASOUND) customers. The other authors declare no conflicts of interest.</p></sec></body><back><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="jcu23886-sec-0022"><title>Data Availability Statement</title><p>The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.</p></sec><ref-list content-type="cited-references" id="jcu23886-bibl-0001"><title>References</title><ref id="jcu23886-bib-0001"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="jcu23886-cit-0001">
